From: EglantinePS5
Subject: up: RangeSet as one sorted array with binary search (EDEN_UP_RANGESET_FLAT), plus subtract and query-cache erase skips, off by default

What: the buffer cache asks its RangeSets (gpu-modified, cpu-written, download, landing ... ranges)
tens of thousands of questions a frame on the GPU back thread, most of which miss. Our RangeSet is a
boost interval set whose tree nodes come from a pool behind a mutex. With EDEN_UP_RANGESET_FLAT=1
every RangeSet keeps its ranges sorted and joined in one std::vector and answers Add, Subtract,
Contains, Intersects, ForEach and ForEachInRange with a binary search over contiguous memory, no
allocation per range. Semantics as before, including the joining of touching ranges and our
Removals() counter (it still counts every Subtract, Clear and move, hit or miss).
OverlapRangeSet (counts per segment, heap mapper) is not touched.

Switch: EDEN_UP_RANGESET_FLAT=1 (exact "1"), read once when the first RangeSet is made. Both
containers are compiled in; each RangeSet reads the switch into a const bool at construction and
every call tests it. Off: the interval set's code as before after that one test, plus an empty
std::vector (24 bytes) in each set. On: one line "EDEN_UP_RANGESET_FLAT on: RangeSet is a sorted
array" on the C stream stderr (stderr.log).

Also in this patch, always on (provably behaviour-neutral), the half of zortos293/Lichii #412 we
did not have:
- RangeSet::Subtract (interval-set path) returns after the Removals count when Intersects says the
  range overlaps nothing: a subtract of a range that overlaps nothing changes nothing. The flat
  array's own subtract starts with the same lookup.
- QueryCacheBase::IterateCache erases a page's queries only when the scan found one in the range:
  erase_if with no match removes nothing.
#412's other parts are ours already: pipeline once is 0400 (EDEN_GBB_PIPELINE_ONCE), the
usage-tracker reset skip is 0064. Not duplicated.

Differences from #481: ForEachInRange has no early return for an empty query, so a query of size 0
strictly inside a range calls back (start, start) as the interval set does. One known difference
stays: ForEachInRange(0, 0) on the interval set hands back every range as an inverted pair
(lower, 0) (its discrete interval [0,0) has last() = ~0); the flat array hands back none. Device
address 0 with size 0 is not a query any caller makes on purpose. #481's catch2 test is not taken
(our core has no tests folder); a Mac test compares both containers instead (below).

Check (Mac, t140b/test/rangeset_equal.cpp): the T137 RangeSet against this one, switch off and on,
on random add / subtract / clear / move / self-move sequences (u64 and u32, spaces 512 to 65,536,
sizes 0 to the whole space, ends that wrap), after every step: same range list, Empty, Removals,
the per-address model, no touching ranges, and 6 random Contains / Intersects / ForEachInRange
queries. 3,036,315 checks, 0 mismatches both ways, and under ASan and UBSan with the switch on.
Built with the classic libc++ tree search (_LIBCPP_ENABLE_LEGACY_TREE_LOWER_UPPER_BOUND): the
Mac's libc++ 22 stops lower_bound at any overlapping node, which breaks the interval set's
range walk on the Mac only; the PS5 build's libc++ 18.1 has the classic search.

Origin and credit: zortos293/Lichii #481 (47a1f19d54, Marc Stampfli), the sorted-array RangeSet,
taken line for line inside the switch; #412 (079941fc6f, Zortos, from Frizti), the subtract and
query-cache erase skips. Author's numbers (PC, Ocarina village save, back thread the limit):
RangeSet 5.0% -> 2.8% of the back thread, standing 42.7 -> 43.9 fps, camera pan 33.6 -> 34.7;
#412's half about 3% of the back thread (Frizti's number).
